{
  "reviews": [
    {
      "id": "b200-adaptive-verification-1",
      "criteria": {
        "task_value": {
          "verdict": "unknown",
          "score": 3,
          "rationale": "The cheap flag inspection can resolve whether activation is a real baseline delta: the brief says “CSV dspark_config off contradicts profile on; runtime flags not inspected.” The same-topology experiment targets the required cost and latency metrics. The target verification bottleneck and benefit remain unknown."
        },
        "correctness_and_safety": {
          "verdict": "unknown",
          "score": 2,
          "rationale": "The proposal retains draft k5 and calls for sampling, KV rollback, and graph-safe decode checks. Safety is unqualified: issue #59970 reports “IMA within 1 min at c96 (4/4)” on B300, while PR #59986 says its end-to-end reproducer was “Not run.” Neither establishes safety on this B200 deployment."
        },
        "causal_test": {
          "verdict": "pass",
          "score": 5,
          "rationale": "It compares adaptive k5 with static k5, static k2, and no speculation on one frozen topology and fixed traces. It specifies both goodput scorers, TTFT/E2E tails, ITL length cohorts, correctness, and a separate safety change. The reported R7b and R10 results cannot isolate speculation because their layouts and load points differ."
        },
        "implementation_evidence": {
          "verdict": "unknown",
          "score": 3,
          "rationale": "The supplied issue and PR bodies identify relevant graph builders and a proposed safety change, but deployed code and flags have not been inspected. PR #62541 says B200 qualified lanes use “enable_adaptive_verification:false”; that author claim supports testing the choice, not assuming activation helps."
        }
      },
      "factual_support": "pass",
      "objections": [
        "Author-reported negative evidence is stronger than the draft’s short quotes convey: #59970 says “Throughput was the same within noise” for its workaround comparison, and #62541 reports fixed full-block verification in qualified B200 lanes.",
        "The #59970 failure occurred on 2×B300 with TP2 and a different workload; transfer to 8×B200 is unknown.",
        "The claimed safety fix is an open PR whose B300 end-to-end reproducer was not run. Exact sampling distribution and KV rollback on the proposed build are unknown.",
        "PR #45963 concerns batch-local dynamic draft K under DP. Keeping draft k5 fixed avoids that documented divergence mechanism, but does not establish adaptive verification safety."
      ],
      "required_revision": "Keep this as the first, deferred hypothesis. Inspect effective flags and freeze the checkout, patch, and container; establish graph-safe concurrent-prefill behavior and correctness before the bounded same-topology performance comparison. State #59970’s throughput result and #62541’s fixed-k5 choice explicitly."
    },
    {
      "id": "b200-schedulable-cost-calibration-2",
      "criteria": {
        "task_value": {
          "verdict": "unknown",
          "score": 2,
          "rationale": "Issue #54046 describes a concrete profiling mismatch: “Anything the profiler measures above 1024 tokens cannot be decode” for its k7, seq128 example. Whether the frozen k5 runtime has the behavior, reaches relevant tail shapes, changes decisions, or improves target cost and latency is unknown. The proposed code-presence and shape checks are cheap."
        },
        "correctness_and_safety": {
          "verdict": "unknown",
          "score": 2,
          "rationale": "The proposal preserves draft k5 and makes graph safety and adaptive eligibility prerequisites. PR #54077 claims it changes startup profiling shapes and “does not modify sampling or verification kernels,” but target sampling, rollback, and concurrent-prefill safety have not been qualified."
        },
        "causal_test": {
          "verdict": "pass",
          "score": 4,
          "rationale": "The treatment changes profiling alone after activation and safety are frozen. It checks reachable shapes, cost estimates, and decisions before a bounded serving comparison with fixed traces, both scorers, latency tails, ITL cohorts, and correctness. The second-stage comparison remains conditional on the first hypothesis producing an eligible adaptive baseline."
        },
        "implementation_evidence": {
          "verdict": "unknown",
          "score": 3,
          "rationale": "PR #54077 supplies a specific claimed implementation: “profiles tail samples as schedulable mixed decode/prefill batches,” with reported regression tests. It also says “A full DSpark model-serving evaluation was not run.” The pinned runtime’s code path and any equivalent upstream behavior remain uninspected."
        }
      },
      "factual_support": "pass",
      "objections": [
        "The 1024-token ceiling is from a k7, seq128 configuration; the target k5 ceiling must be calculated from resolved runtime limits and checked against actual scheduled shapes.",
        "PR #54077’s reported tests used an RTX 5090. Its serving effect on the specified 8×B200 workload is unknown.",
        "A corrected profile may leave policy decisions unchanged. No target effect size or active verification-cost bottleneck is supplied.",
        "DScale’s reported A100 TP1 gains and EVICT’s estimator limitations are context, not evidence that this vLLM port improves B200 serving."
      ],
      "required_revision": "Retain only as a conditional second hypothesis. First establish adaptive eligibility and safety, inspect the pinned profiler for equivalent behavior, then verify a reachable shape mismatch and changed decisions before any performance sweep."
    }
  ],
  "global_gaps": [
    "Observed baseline report: R7b c512 is 33.76 req/s with E2E p95 19.49 s; R10 DEP8 k2 c1024 is 43.55 req/s with E2E p95 29.61 s. These are different layouts and load points, so neither is a causal speculation result.",
    "Effective deployed DSpark and adaptive flags, frozen checkout, patch, container, graph path, and presence of the two proposed PR behaviors are unknown.",
    "Target verification cost share, acceptance distribution, mixed-batch shapes, decision changes, safety, and performance effect sizes are unknown. The proposed 5% threshold is a decision rule, not an observed gain.",
    "The supplied DScale excerpt reports A100 TP1, concurrency 8–32, and paper-author gains, but its transfer to B200 DSpark k5 is unknown. The supplied EVICT excerpt identifies domain-shift and large-batch limitations; its target hardware and target effect size are unknown. The supplied speculative-decoding paper conclusion claims verification is dominant in its study, but the excerpt does not establish that bottleneck on this workload.",
    "Historical novelty rejections do not veto either deployment candidate; novelty is disabled by the rubric."
  ]
}
